  "title": "Is Liquid Death sustainable or is it greenwashing? 💦 Welcome to “sust...",
  "description": "Is Liquid Death sustainable or is it greenwashing? 💦 Welcome to “sustainable or greenwashing month” where everyday leading up to Earth Month I’m sharing if brands and products are actually what they say they are. 👩🏼‍💻 Liquid Death was founded in 2018 to offer a different perspective on canned beverages that weren’t just energy drinks & it’s really gained popularity due to its edgy marketing techniques.  One of their overarching goals and campaigns is #deathtoplastic - which you guessed it - advocates for the end of plastic pollution. The water comes in a tallboy aluminum cans which are infinitely recyclable & fun fact - of all the aluminum produced since 1888, 75% of it is still currently in use. ♻️ They’ve started the Liquid Death Country Club to “sell your soul” and for every soul they “sell”, they pledge a donation of $1.80 to plastic cleanup efforts. They also partner with non-profit environmental organizations to assist with beach clean-ups and climate change efforts like 5 Gyres & Thirst Project. For sourcing of ingredients, I couldn’t find too much especially since they’ve expanding into a lot of different flavors and more recently teas. I hope in the future there is more transparency there. I will say their website is not very descriptive, so I had to really deep dive to clarify some of this information, so I wish they were better on that - but overall, the brand itself seems to be sustainable - but what do you think? 👀 This month we’re doing an ENTIRE month of Sustainable or Greenwashing videos, so if there is a brand you want to see, stick around because it’s probably coming next!  🌎 @Liquid Death  #sustainableorgreenwashing #liquiddeath",
